<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhanga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 950–<a href="/years/999">999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhanga was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandela-dynasty">Chandela dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a> from about 950 to 999. Son of Yashovarman, he threw off the overlordship of the Gurjara-Pratiharas and made the Chandelas a leading power of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He built the Vishvanatha temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khajuraho">Khajuraho</a>, recorded in an inscription of his reign.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Dhanga, called Dhaṇga-deva in inscriptions, was the son of the Chandela king Yashovarman and Queen Puppā Devī. The earliest inscription of his reign, at Chaturbhuj, dates from 953–954 CE, and his accession was probably undisputed, since his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> had been sent to guard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> frontier. That inscription places his kingdom between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinjar-fort">Kalanjara</a>, Bhasvat on the Malava river, the Kalindi River, the Chedi region and the Gopa hill (Gwalior). He styled himself Kalanjaradhipati, Lord of Kalanjara, but appears to have kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khajuraho">Khajuraho</a> as his capital.</p><p>His inscriptions no longer name the Pratiharas as overlords. The Kachchhapaghatas who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwalior-fort">Gwalior Fort</a> were probably his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and helped him against the Pratiharas, and the Mau inscription claims he defeated the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>. The 16th-century historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a> says the Raja of Kalinjar, identified with Dhanga, sent troops to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapala">Jayapala</a> of the Hindu Kabul Shahi dynasty against the Ghaznavids. Court claims of victories as far as Andhra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a> and Simhala appear to be poetic exaggeration, but a campaign in the east may have weakened the Kambojas who had usurped the Pala kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dhanga was a Shaivite. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khajuraho">Khajuraho</a> inscription records that he installed two lingas, one of emerald and one of ordinary stone, in the temple identified as the Vishvanatha. He completed the Vaikuntha-natha Vishnu temple begun by his father, and a Jain temple received gardens from Pahilla, a man Dhanga respected. His chief minister was the Brahmin Prabhasa, appointed after tests set out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a>, and he performed the costly Tulapurusha gift-giving ceremony.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khajuraho">Khajuraho</a> inscription says that after living more than a hundred autumns he attained moksha by abandoning his body in the waters of the Ganga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>. Some scholars read this as suicide, while Rajendralal Mitra took it as a conventional way of announcing a death. He was succeeded by his son Ganda-deva.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dhanga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yashovarman made the dynasty a regional power in the mid-10th century, capturing the fortress of Kalinjar, and his son <b>Dhanga</b> became fully independent.</blockquote><a href="/state/chandela-dynasty">Chandela dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
